<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Michael VIII Palaiologos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1224–<a href="/years/1282">1282</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Michael VIII · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palaiologos-dynasty">Palaiologos dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Michael VIII Palaiologos was a Byzantine emperor who reigned from 1259 to 1282 and founded the Palaiologan dynasty. In 1261 his forces recaptured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-empire">Latin Empire</a>, restoring the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>. However, the restored state was much reduced and faced continual threats.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos.jpg" alt="Michael VIII Palaiologos" width="233" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Pachymeres · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Miniature_of_Michael_VIII.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Michael VIII Palaiologos was the son of the megas domestikos Andronikos Doukas Komnenos Palaiologos and Theodora Angelina Palaiologina, whose ancestry, according to Deno John Geanakoplos, went back to all three imperial houses that ruled before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1204. He governed the Thracian towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melnik">Melnik</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serres">Serres</a> under his father. In 1253 he was accused of plotting against Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-doukas-vatatzes">John III Vatatzes</a> and escaped trial by ordeal with a clever reply, though he remained mistrusted. He served the Seljuk Sultanate of Rum from late 1256 to 1258 before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-ii-laskaris">Theodore II Laskaris</a> recalled him. After Theodore's death in 1258 Michael seized the guardianship of the eight-year-old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iv-laskaris">John IV Laskaris</a> from George Mouzalon and was proclaimed emperor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nymphaion">Nymphaion</a> on 1 January 1259.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1259 Michael defeated William of Villehardouin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-ii-komnenos-doukas">Michael II Komnenos Doukas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pelagonia">Battle of Pelagonia</a>. A treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> followed in 1261, though Genoese help proved unneeded when his general Alexios Strategopoulos captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> from Baldwin II through treachery on 25 July 1261. Michael entered the city on 15 August. He abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> customs, restored Byzantine ceremonies and repopulated the capital, which grew from about 35,000 people to 70,000 by the end of his reign. In December 1261 he had John IV blinded and sent to a monastery, and Patriarch Arsenios excommunicated him for it until 1268.</p><p>From 1266 Michael's main opponent was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou">Charles of Anjou</a>, who in 1267 concluded the Treaty of Viterbo to restore the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-empire">Latin Empire</a>. To restrain him Michael negotiated union of the churches, which was formally proclaimed at the Second Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> in 1274. Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> bitterly opposed it, and Michael punished anti-unionists, some by blinding or exile. On 30 March 1282 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicilian-vespers">Sicilian Vespers</a> rebellion broke out before Charles could sail against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. Pope Martin IV excommunicated Michael, whom many saw as instigator, though Geanakoplos holds that he had nothing to do with the incident at the church of Santo Spirito.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Michael died at Pachomios village in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> on 11 December 1282. He was denied burial in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> because of his persecution of the Church in support of union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. In restoring the empire he kept the old administration without correcting its failures. His focus on Constantinople and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> left the Anatolian frontier stripped of troops, which led to its gradual collapse as Turkish bands infiltrated. The Palaiologan dynasty he founded ruled for almost two centuries, longer than any other in Roman history.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Michael VIII Palaiologos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Palaiologan restoration of 1261, achieved by <b>Michael VIII</b> from the successor state of Nicaea, recovered Constantinople but not the empire's substance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1282 John II married Eudokia, daughter of <b>Michael VIII Palaiologos</b>, and adopted a more modest title, Emperor and Autocrat of all the East, the Iberians and the Perateia, the last term referring to dependencies across the Black Sea in the Crimea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-trebizond">Empire of Trebizond · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Nicaean victory at the Battle of Pelagonia in 1259, only the Theodosian walls stood between <b>Michael VIII Palaiologos</b> and the city.</blockquote><a href="/empire/latin-empire">Latin Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>To win his release in 1262 he ceded Monemvasia, Mistras and Grand Magne to <b>Michael VIII Palaiologos</b>, giving the Byzantines a foothold from which they waged a long war of reconquest.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Treaty of Nymphaeum of 1261 with the Byzantine emperor <b>Michael VIII Palaiologos</b>, Genoa gained privileged access to the Black Sea, its colony at Pera faced Constantinople, and Caffa in the Crimea became a hub for grain, fish, furs and slaves from the steppe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Republic of Genoa · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>George Mouzalon, a high official of the Empire of Nicaea under Theodore II Laskaris and its regent, was assassinated in a conspiracy of nobles led by <b>Michael VIII Palaiologos</b>, who became co-emperor in 1259 and recovered Constantinople in 1261.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-25">25 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Empire of Nicaea. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42,26&amp;year=1261" aria-label="Thrace on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Michael VIII Palaiologos"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4746 3977h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4746 3977h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=42,26&amp;year=1261">What was here in 1261? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
